  "worked_solution": "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-2\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=2\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm 2\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $10.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $10.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10({x}^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+40 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
